  "answer": "To understand anaerobic respiration in the school laboratory, you can follow these steps: First, prepare a glucose solution and heat it for a minute to remove dissolved oxygen, then cool it without shaking. Next, add yeast to the cooled glucose solution. To prevent oxygen from entering, pour a layer of liquid paraffin on top of the mixture. This creates an anaerobic environment. To check if oxygen has been removed, add a few drops of diazine green (Janus Green B) solution to the yeast suspension before adding the paraffin; the dye turns pink when oxygen is in short supply. Attach a bottle containing bicarbonate/indicator solution (or lime water) to the setup. During anaerobic respiration, carbon dioxide is released and turns the lime water milky. You can also observe the temperature change using a thermometer. For a smaller scale experiment, use test tubes and warm them to about 37°C to speed up the process.",
